erally considered more flexible and stronger.\n\nReason: **N: g1-f3** is the strongest move because:\n- It develops a piece with tempo (controls center)\n- Prepares kingside castling for king safety\n- Maintains flexibility in pawn structure\n- Follows the opening principle: "Knights before bishops"\n- Puts pressure on Black\'s position and prepares for multiple pawn breaks (d4, d3)\n- The knight on f3 is optimally placed, controlling key central squares\n\nThis move aligns with classical opening theory and maintains White\'s slight initiative from the first move advantage.\n\nAnswer: N: g1-f3'}
