sn't capitalize on the tactical opportunity at f7.\n\nReason: The move **B: c4-f7** is clearly the strongest. It wins the f7 pawn with check - a forcing move that Black must respond to. After Bxf7+, Black's king will be displaced (either Ke7 or Kxf7), ruining their castling rights and exposing their king. This is a fundamental tactical pattern in chess openings where f7 is often the weakest point in Black's position early in the game. Material gain combined with king safety damage gives White a significant advantage.\n\nAnswer: B: c4-f7"}
