\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, Q: e8-d7, Q: e8-c6, Q: e8-b5, Q: e8-a4, Q: e8-d8, Q: e8-c8, Q: e8-e7, Q: e8-f7, Q: e8-g6, Q: e8-h5, Q: e8-f8, Q: e8-g8, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as White and have a massive material advantage - I captured Black's queen and it's now on my side at e8! Black's king is exposed on e6 with limited escape squares. Black's pieces are somewhat uncoordinated with pawns on a5, b6, c4, e7, g5, h6 and pieces on the back rank mostly unmoved.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider the most forcing moves:\n\n1. **Q: e8-c6+** - This gives check, attacking the king on e6. The king would be forced to move, likely to d5, e5, or f5. This maintains pressure and keeps the initiative.\n\n2. **Q: e8-e7+** - Also check, but the king can escape more easily and this seems less controlling.\n\n3. **Q: e8-h5** - Attacks g5 pawn and h6 pawn, but doesn't give check. Less forcing.\n\n4. Pawn moves or developing moves - These are too slow given my overwhelming advantage.\n\nThe key principle here is to use checks to restrict Black's king and prevent any counterplay. With such a large material advantage (extra queen), I should force the issue and drive toward checkmate or further material gain.\n\nReason: Q: e8-c6+ is the strongest move because:\n- It gives check, forcing Black's response\n- The queen on c6 controls many central squares\n- It limits the Black king's mobility \n- After the king moves, I can continue with more checks or capture hanging pawns\n- This methodical approach with checks will lead to a quick victory\n\nAnswer: Q: e8-c6"}
