\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as White in what appears to be a mid-game position. Black's king has been forced to c5, which is quite exposed in the center of the board. Black has developed knights to d6 and g8, with rooks still on their starting squares (a8 and h8). Black's pawns are scattered but some are advanced. On my side, I have most of my kingside pieces developed (rook on f1, king on g1 which has moved), but my queenside is still relatively undeveloped. My pawn structure is mostly intact on the second rank.\n\nEvaluation: The critical feature here is Black's exposed king on c5. Let me consider promising moves:\n\n1. **N: b1-c3** - Develops the knight and puts immediate check on the Black king at c5. This forces Black's king to move and gains tempo while developing.\n\n2. **N: b1-a3** - Develops the knight but to a less active square, doesn't create immediate threats.\n\n3. **P: d2-d4** - Opens up the center and potentially attacks the Black king's escape squares, but doesn't create immediate pressure.\n\n4. **P: b2-b4+** - Wait, this would give check to the king on c5! However, I don't see this in my valid moves list, so it must not be legal from the current position.\n\n5. **R: f1-e1** or **R: f1-d1** - Activates the rook but doesn't create immediate threats.\n\nReason: **N: b1-c3** is the strongest move because it develops my knight with check (the knight on c3 attacks the Black king on c5). This forces Black to move their exposed king, giving me tempo to continue development while Black's king remains vulnerable. Developing with tempo is a fundamental chess principle, and checking the opponent's king while developing is particularly strong.\n\nAnswer: N: b1-c3"}
